Day 3: Kubernetes Basics, Rollouts and Troubleshooting
Nov 1, 2026 · @Srinivas BV
On Day 3 a three-replica nginx Deployment on GKE was healed, scaled, updated, broken on purpose and rolled back, and two connection errors were diagnosed along the way.
What Day 3 covered
The goal was to learn how a Deployment behaves under normal operation and under failure, using a fresh GKE cluster created and destroyed the same night.
•	Setup: terraform apply recreated the zonal cluster sre-ml-gke (2 Spot nodes, 9 resources). Manifests in k8s/day3/: a namespace, a Deployment web (nginx, 3 replicas, rolling update with maxSurge: 1 and maxUnavailable: 0, readiness and liveness probes, requests and limits) and a ClusterIP Service.
•	Experiments: self-healing, scaling 3 to 5 and back, a good rolling update (1.27 to 1.28), a bad image rollout (nginx:1.99.99), a bad readiness probe (path /nope) and a rollback after each failure.
•	Result: the site stayed available through every failure, because the old pods were never removed until new ones were ready. Every rollback restored the last good ReplicaSet.
•	Side findings: two connection errors (x509 and i/o timeout), and drift between Git and the live cluster.
Lab observations
Each experiment showed one Kubernetes behaviour directly in the command output.
Experiment	What was observed	Why it happened
Initial deploy	3 pods 1/1 Running, spread 2 and 1 across the 2 nodes. Pod IPs 10.24.1.x on one node and 10.24.0.x on the other	The scheduler balances by requests. Each node owns its own pod IP range
Delete all pods	3 new pods within about a second, same ReplicaSet hash 6f798b7ccb	The ReplicaSet restores the desired count. The template did not change, so the same ReplicaSet recreated them
New pods after creation	Running but 0/1 for 5 to 15 seconds	The readiness probe had not passed yet. Running does not mean ready
Scale 3 to 5 to 3	Extra pods spread over both nodes (2 and 3). Scale-down removed the extras	Default scheduling spreads roughly, not strictly
Rolling update 1.27 to 1.28	One new pod, wait for ready, remove one old pod, repeated 3 times. Peak 4 pods, never fewer than 3 ready. New ReplicaSet 8449bf6b9, old kept at 0	maxSurge: 1 allows one extra pod. maxUnavailable: 0 keeps ready pods at 3. The old ReplicaSet is kept for rollback
Bad image nginx:1.99.99	New pod ErrImagePull then ImagePullBackOff. The 3 old pods stayed 1/1. rollout status timed out. Event: NotFound	Tag does not exist in the registry. The new pod never became ready, so no old pod was removed
Bad readiness path /nope	New pod Running, 0/1, RESTARTS 0. Event: Readiness probe failed: statuscode 404. Old pods stayed 1/1	Container healthy but failing its check, so it gets no traffic and is not restarted
rollout undo (twice)	Previous ReplicaSet scaled back to 3. Revision numbers moved forward (1, 3, 4)	A rollback reuses an old template as a new revision
Two extra findings:
•	Drift: kubectl set image and rollout undo change the cluster but not the YAML in Git. The repo says nginx:1.27 while the cluster ran nginx:1.28, and kubectl warned about the last-applied-configuration annotation.
•	Misleading history: CHANGE-CAUSE is copied from an annotation at each revision. Revision 3 (the bad image) showed the same text as revision 4 because the annotation was not updated for each change.
Troubleshooting log
Four problems came up. Two were real cluster-access errors, and two were typing slips that made the fix look like it failed.
1. x509: certificate signed by unknown authority
•	Symptom: every kubectl command failed with tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate signed by unknown authority.
•	Root cause: each GKE cluster generates its own certificate authority (CA). get-credentials stores the endpoint and CA in ~/.kube/config. The cluster was destroyed on Day 2 and recreated with the same name, so the kubeconfig still held the old CA. The new API server presented a certificate signed by a different CA, and kubectl correctly refused it.
•	Diagnosis: kubectl config current-context showed the right name, which hid the problem. Comparing the CA in kubeconfig with gcloud container clusters describe would show a mismatch.
•	Fix: gcloud container clusters get-credentials sre-ml-gke --zone asia-south1-a --project srevert-sri023, then kubectl get nodes listed both nodes Ready.
•	Prevention: run get-credentials first after every apply, delete the stale context and cluster entries after every destroy, and never use --insecure-skip-tls-verify.
2. The fix that did not run
•	Symptom: the x509 error stayed after the suggested refresh.
•	Root cause: the command was pasted as (terraform output ...) without the leading $, so the shell printed the command text instead of running it. A later paste also joined two words (kubectlkubectl).
•	Fix: run the gcloud ... get-credentials command directly and check for kubeconfig entry generated.
•	Prevention: paste small blocks, read them before pressing Enter, and verify each fix by its output.
3. dial tcp ...:443: i/o timeout (twice)
•	Symptom: the first kubectl command after a pause failed with an i/o timeout to the control-plane IP. The retry worked both times.
•	Root cause: a brief network issue between the laptop and the cluster's public endpoint. It is a different failure class from x509: the connection never completed, rather than completing with an untrusted certificate. Not confirmed beyond that.
•	Fix: retry the command.
•	Prevention: if it repeats, check the connection or VPN, and test reachability of the endpoint. The control-plane IP also changes on every recreate (8.234.120.90 on Day 2, 8.234.67.35 on Day 3).
4. Event commands returning nothing
•	Symptom: kubectl describe pod -l app=web | grep ... printed nothing, even though the failure existed.
•	Root cause: filtering describe output with grep over several pods is unreliable, and probe events appear a few seconds after the first failure.
•	Fix: kubectl describe pod <name> -n day3 | tail -15 and kubectl get events -n day3 --sort-by=.lastTimestamp.
•	Prevention: use get events for the timeline and describe on one named pod for detail.
Failure patterns
READY and RESTARTS tell you more than STATUS: Running does not mean healthy.
Symptom	Container running?	Typical cause	First check
ImagePullBackOff or ErrImagePull	No, never started	Wrong tag, missing image, registry access	describe pod events
Running, 0/1, restarts 0	Yes	Readiness probe failing: wrong path or port, app not ready	get events, app logs
CrashLoopBackOff, restarts climbing	Starts, then exits	App crash, bad config, or liveness probe failing	logs --previous
Pending	No	No node fits the requests, or a scheduling constraint	describe pod events
OOMKilled	Killed and restarted	Memory over the limit	describe pod, last state
Diagnosis routine for any stuck pod: kubectl get pods for status, kubectl describe pod <name> for events, kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp for the cluster timeline, then kubectl logs <name> for the application.
The last three rows were not seen tonight and are listed from general Kubernetes knowledge.
Interview questions
These are built from what happened tonight, so each answer can use a real example. Practise answering aloud in under two minutes. The points under each question are what a strong answer covers.
Core concepts
1.	What happens if a pod in a Deployment dies?
o	The ReplicaSet notices the count is below replicas and creates a replacement. It is a pod, not a node.
o	Example: all 3 pods were deleted and 3 new ones were running within about a second.
2.	Explain readiness and liveness probes. What happens when each fails?
o	Readiness failing removes the pod from Service endpoints, with no restart.
o	Liveness failing makes the kubelet restart the container.
o	Example: a bad readiness path left a pod Running 0/1 with RESTARTS 0.
3.	What is the difference between requests and limits?
o	Requests are reserved and used by the scheduler to place the pod. Limits are enforced at runtime.
o	Over the memory limit, the container is OOMKilled. Over the CPU limit, it is throttled.
o	Mention QoS: requests below limits gives Burstable, equal gives Guaranteed.
4.	What do maxSurge and maxUnavailable control?
o	maxSurge: 1 allows one pod above the desired count. maxUnavailable: 0 keeps ready pods at the desired count.
o	Together: start a new pod, wait for ready, remove an old one, repeat.
5.	Why use a Service when pods already have IPs?
o	Pod IPs change when pods are recreated. A Service gives a stable virtual IP and DNS name and load-balances to ready pods selected by label.
Scenario and troubleshooting
6.	A rollout is stuck at 1 of 3 new replicas. What do you do?
o	kubectl rollout status, get pods, describe pod, get events.
o	Decide by symptom: ImagePullBackOff means a bad image reference, and Running 0/1 means a failing readiness check.
o	If the new version cannot be fixed quickly, kubectl rollout undo, then fix and redeploy.
7.	Why did users stay unaffected during your bad deployment?
o	maxUnavailable: 0 forbids removing an old pod until a new one is ready. The new pod never became ready, so all 3 old pods kept serving.
8.	How do you tell ImagePullBackOff from a failing readiness probe?
o	Compare the table above: whether the container runs, the READY column, restarts and the events.
o	Strong answer adds the third pattern, CrashLoopBackOff with climbing restarts.
9.	kubectl returns x509: certificate signed by unknown authority after you recreated a cluster. What is the cause?
o	Every GKE cluster has its own CA. The kubeconfig still has the old one, so verification fails.
o	Confirm by comparing the CA in kubeconfig with the cluster's. Fix with get-credentials. Do not skip TLS verification.
10.	How is that error different from dial tcp ... i/o timeout?
o	x509: the connection completed but trust failed. Timeout: the connection never completed, so look at the network path.
Design and judgment
11.	What is drift, and how did you create it?
o	kubectl set image and rollout undo changed the live cluster but not the YAML in Git, so Git said 1.27 while the cluster ran 1.28, and a later kubectl apply would silently revert it.
o	GitOps tools such as ArgoCD make Git the source of truth and report or correct drift.
12.	How would you make this rollout safer in production?
o	Add a startupProbe for slow apps, a PodDisruptionBudget, topologySpreadConstraints across nodes or zones, and image tags pinned by digest.
o	Automate checks: run canary analysis (planned for Week 6) and roll back on failure.
o	Set the change-cause automatically in CI so the revision history is accurate.
13.	Why do you destroy the cluster after every session, and how do you protect against runaway cost?
o	Terraform makes recreation cheap and repeatable. Budget alerts only notify, so destruction is the real control.
o	Mention the free-trial credit and its expiry.
Behavioural
14.	Tell me about a time you debugged something under pressure.
o	Use the x509 story: symptom, hypothesis (stale CA), how you confirmed it, the fix, and the prevention rule you added.
o	Be honest about the slip: a mistyped command made the first fix look like it failed, and the lesson was to verify every fix by its output.

